WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.310
You really can’t tell the meaning of someone’s
life till the very end.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:41.700
And it’s for the same reason, right, is
that how all the pieces fit together in the

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.500
story or in the life is not necessarily determined
until the final moment.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.560
And I think that’s part of the reason too,
why among Catholics, for example, and Christians

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.930
in general, there’s an idea that salvation
can always be attained, right?

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.330
Right up to the last moment, no matter what
your life was like.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.660
And you think: Well, that’s a pretty cheap
trick, you know, because you can run around

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.400
doing terrible things your whole life, but
as long as you can get it together at the

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.080
last second, then you’re Scot-free.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.050
But if you think about it in terms of a story,
then you can understand how that could conceivably

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:11.860
be the case.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.770
And it’s for this reason, of course, that
this lecture in particular makes me nervous

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:20.160
more than any of the other ones I do, because
I’ve been telling you a story that’s basically

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.500
40 hours long, right, and its spoken forum,
and then who knows how long in its written

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:26.500
form.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.290
And it’s complicated to pull it together
properly.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:34.649
And that’s partly because, as far as I’ve
been concerned, we’ve been talking about

00:01:34.649 --> 00:01:38.210
issues in psychology that are more difficult
than any other.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:42.250
First of all, conceptually, even from a neuropsychological
perspective, because I’ve been offering

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:47.450
you a model of the way the brain processes
the environment, that I think is really novel.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.040
And I think it reflects the current state
of neuroscience.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.230
And - but more than that, there’s the problem
that we’ve been dealing with issues all

00:01:55.230 --> 00:02:01.260
the way along of life and death, and of war
and destruction, and of the possibility for

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:02.820
clear-headed optimism, right?

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:08.729
and possibility which, as we’ve discussed,
more or less escaped Tolstoy, say, for most

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:13.780
of his life, right, because when Tolstoy woke
up from his delusions, he looked at the world

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:20.310
and he said: Well, clearly it’s such a terrible
place that, if you’re not looking at it

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:24.760
through the veils of illusion, there’s no
way that you can do anything but stand in

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.730
opposition to it once you understand its basic
structure, right?

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:34.410
Suffering and innocence suffering, and complete
vulnerability, and the whole existential mess

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:35.950
that makes up life.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:45.770
Now it turns out that Tolstoy overcame his
rationally induced cynicism in a kind of mystical

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.459
way.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:53.470
He had a dream that he was suspended from
some transcendent space by a belt around the

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.660
middle of his waist, which hung him over a
pit of chaos.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:59.380
And in that image, he found comfort.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.760
And fair enough, it’s a powerful image.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.269
But it’s not well delineated, right?

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:09.110
And it worked well for Tolstoy, and you can
see that the image has power, but you can’t--

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:11.079
you can’t grab it with your rational mind.

00:03:11.079 --> 00:03:13.959
You can’t take it into pieces and analyze
it as an argument.

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:16.950
And that’s what we do if we’re intellectuals,
right?

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:21.920
We try to understand the detailed structure
of something.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:28.260
And I think the detailed structure of what
Tolstoy apprehended as optimistic is actually

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.260
comprehensible.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:36.070
And we’ve been working towards that and
circling around it the entire course of this

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:38.160
lecture series.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.910
But I have found with this material that with
each circling around the target, it gets clearer

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:43.910
and clearer.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:44.910
It’s a funny thing.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:49.120
It’s like you’re looking at something
that’s too complicated to see all at once.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.540
So you have to look at it from multiple different
perspectives, and again and again.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.600
And each time you look at it, it becomes clearer
and clearer.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.260
And that’s still the case for me when I
go through this material.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.750
Every time I go through it, I think: Oh yeah,
that piece fits there.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:01.880
And that piece fits there.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.950
And that’s how that makes sense.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:07.360
And: Oh that’s a lot more remarkable than
I thought it was to begin with… and so on.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:08.940
And it seems fundamentally inexhaustible.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.250
And of course, that’s what you’d expect
from deep, deep stories, right?

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:16.340
Stories that have been around for thousands
and thousands of years, wouldn’t have been

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:21.100
around for thousands and thousands of years
unless they were in some sense inexhaustible.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:27.870
And we’ve talked about some of the processes
that might contribute to that inexhaustibility.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:32.850
So at the beginning of the lecture series,
I told you to consider the assumption that

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.560
there was more than one way of looking at
the world, right?

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:40.479
There was the standard materialist sort of
scientific viewpoint, which was that the world

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:46.840
was made up of objects – independently existent,
as among them - the objects have no intrinsic

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.650
value, one way or another.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:54.230
And the issue of meaning per se wasn’t included
in that account.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:57.280
And then I said: Well wait, there’s another
way of looking at the world, that we spent

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:02.770
just as much time developing, that we utilize
even more, and that’s the narrative…

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:08.310
That’s the narrative way of looking at the
world; to consider the nature of experience

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:13.190
rather than the nature of objects as real;
to consider your experience as real, even

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:17.220
though it includes things that can’t be
easily and tangibly identified – things

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:22.650
like emotions, which of course you find compelling,
sometimes even beyond your will.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:29.410
Things like motivational states, fantasies,
ideals, all the things that compel your behaviour

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:33.430
and give you some sense that there’s a direction
to life.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:39.160
people who study emotion and perception have
come to understand that the act of transforming

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:43.480
the world into something simply made out of
objects is incredibly difficult.

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:46.900
It’s so difficult that we haven’t been
able to design machines that can do it at

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:47.900
all.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.790
It turns out also that when we look at the
world, we’re not just looking at it with

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:55.250
our visual systems, but we’re looking at
it with our motor output systems and our emotions.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:06:00.180
So that, when you look at something like a
chair, which just stands there for you like

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:05.961
an object, it turns out that the mechanical
systems – the motor systems – that you

00:06:05.961 --> 00:06:10.010
would use to use the chair to sit on it are
activated during the act of perception.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:13.620
It also turns out sometimes that when you
look at something, especially if it’s something

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:19.620
you don’t understand and that it’s - and
that scares you – you actually react to

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:24.490
it; conceptualize it with your body and with
your emotions before you have any idea what

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:31.640
it is from the perspective of an object.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:36.110
When you look at the world and when you think
about the world, you have to do it from a

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:39.470
motivated perspective and an emotionally ridden
perspective.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:44.140
You can’t even see the world without being
gripped by your motivation and emotional states.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:51.300
And so, the idea that rationality or perception
is somehow separate from, or super-ordinate

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:56.120
to perception and emotion is just wrong.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:00.080
We understand now that you can’t even think
without being motivated.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.160
You can’t see the world without being motivated.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.270
And that means you always look at the world
through a kind of lens.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:09.400
And the lens is a narrowing lens, and it has
to be because the world is so complicated,

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.620
you can’t see it all at once.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:15.250
You’re only seeing tiny slices of it in
time, and tiny slices of it in space.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:20.210
And even then, you have to narrow it to only
those things that are relevant to you at that

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:21.210
moment.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:22.530
And we don’t know exactly how you do that.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:30.200
We know that it takes years and years of perceptual
work in infancy, say so that you manage to

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.560
build up an object conception of the world.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.250
That’s probably all your doing in the first
two or three years of life.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:37.740
And you’re doing it constantly.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:41.930
And it’s just as complicated as learning
language, say, or even more complicated.

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:45.240
Most of it’s invisible, and we don’t know
how children do that.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.380
And by the time they can talk, they’ve already
done it.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.640
So, they can’t even tell us what they’re
doing.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.560
It takes a long time to build up an object
world.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.960
And when you look at the world - when you
go from Point A to Point B - even when you’re

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:02.230
doing something as simple as looking for food
in-in the kitchen, you ignore everything about

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:06.370
the world that isn’t relevant to making
yourself a peanut butter sandwich, and you

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:11.300
focus in on those few things that are – the
refrigerator, the food, the knives, the cutlery,

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:12.300
and so on.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:13.980
And everything else is screened out.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:18.620
Then, you can’t help but look at the world
through that kind of lens.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:23.340
And the lenses change and you can be in different
motivational states, and they can change because

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:24.760
of internal transformations.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:25.790
You’re not hungry.

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:26.790
You’re thirsty.

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:28.870
Or you’re not thirsty, you’re interested
in someone.

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:33.810
Or somebody is telling you a story and then
you adopt their motivational framework, and

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.140
you can see the world through their eyes.

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:39.110
And now, we know the neural machinery for
that, and we already talked about that.

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:44.229
So, we can toss back and forth these motivational
frames of reference and that gives us insight

00:08:44.229 --> 00:08:46.370
into someone else’s world.

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:49.389
You can look at the world endless numbers
of ways.

00:08:49.389 --> 00:08:55.050
And what you’re trying to do it, out of
its infinite richness, so to speak, is to

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:59.070
pull out parts of it that are useful for you
while you’re moving from Point A to Point

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:01.880
B.
And this can be a chair, if you want to sit

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:02.880
down.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:06.410
But if you want to take a light bulb out of
the ceiling, then it’s a stool, or a table.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:11.361
And whether or not it’s a chair, or a stool,
or a table depends just as much on what you’re

00:09:11.361 --> 00:09:15.490
doing as it does on what it is.

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:18.970
And I think that’s part of the reason why
human beings can be so infinitely creative,

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:19.970
right?

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:21.010
For us, the world isn’t fixed.

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:22.759
We never know what it’s going to bring forth.

00:09:22.759 --> 00:09:29.610
So, 100 years ago, if someone would have said:
Well, you could build a machine on a wafer

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:36.149
a centimetre square out of sand and if you
have enough of those machines then everyone

00:09:36.149 --> 00:09:39.870
in the world can be connected, and everyone
in the world can have an infinite library

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:41.649
of verbal material, right?

00:09:41.649 --> 00:09:43.390
That’s impossible, but it-it’s not impossible.

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:51.379
It turns out that silica has those properties,
and we can build unbelievably powerful machines

00:09:51.379 --> 00:09:54.560
out of nothing
And so then, that kind of makes you think

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:58.240
about just what this nothing that we’re
building things out of is, right?

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:05.019
Because it seems to be able to reveal a constant
array of properties; properties that are essentially

00:10:05.019 --> 00:10:06.149
unlimited.

00:10:06.149 --> 00:10:10.920
And it’s capacity to reveal those properties
seems to depend as much on our ability to

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:19.350
interact with it - whatever that is - as it
does on whatever the stuff is.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:25.040
And we know, even from a strict object perception
of the world that the stuff that things are

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:29.490
made of is a lot more complicated than we
had originally presumed, even as materialists,

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:38.069
because materialists, realists, their philosophy
only holds down to about the subatomic level

00:10:38.069 --> 00:10:40.300
of analysis – a deterministic worldview.

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:44.500
Below the subatomic level of analysis, there’s
nothing deterministic at all.

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:48.199
And the stuff that things are made of is so
mysterious that we can’t even-- we can’t

00:10:48.199 --> 00:10:49.199
grasp it.

00:10:49.199 --> 00:10:50.259
We can’t comprehend it.

00:10:50.259 --> 00:10:57.160
So it turns out that rather than the story
world be, being dependent on the object world,

00:10:57.160 --> 00:10:58.240
it might be the other way around.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.680
The object world is dependent on the story
world.

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:05.470
And that implies at least to some degree that
the story world is actually more real, whatever

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:06.470
that means.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:11.939
And then, I told you that, well, the real
problem of life isn’t so much “What do

00:11:11.939 --> 00:11:16.149
you do when you’re around things that you
understand?”

00:11:16.149 --> 00:11:20.360
The real problem of life is: “What do you
do when you encounter something you can’t

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:21.480
conceptualize?”

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:25.610
And I think a good recent example of that
was the bombing of the World Trade Towers,

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:30.210
which people were compelled to watch over
and over and over and over.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:33.100
And if you ask someone: Well, what is it that
you’re watching?

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:37.850
They would say: Well, I’m watching the World
Trade Towers fall down but then, you might

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:39.380
say: Well, why are you watching it over and
over?

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:42.040
And they would say something like: Well, I
can’t believe it.

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.139
I can’t believe it’s happening.

00:11:44.139 --> 00:11:45.640
And what does that mean?

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:50.459
It means something like: Whatever it is that’s
happening here - whatever’s being blown

00:11:50.459 --> 00:11:53.309
apart – exceeds my ability to model.

00:11:53.309 --> 00:11:57.660
And as a consequence, I have to expose myself
to it again and again and again, and again,

00:11:57.660 --> 00:11:59.420
to try to understand what’s falling.

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:00.980
Like, what’s falling exactly?

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:02.040
Is it just the towers?

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:04.190
Is it 20,000 people?

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:06.269
Is it the financial system of the U.S.?

00:12:06.269 --> 00:12:08.339
Is it the stability of the western world?

00:12:08.339 --> 00:12:10.000
Is it the beginning of World War III?

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:16.059
Or as the former CIA director just mentioned
in the U.S.: The beginning of World War IV.

00:12:16.059 --> 00:12:24.990
What is it exactly that you’re looking at
when something happens that you don’t understand?

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:27.029
And then you say: Well, how do you react to
that?

00:12:27.029 --> 00:12:31.870
And it turns out that you react mostly with
your body - not with your mind, not with your

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:35.800
perceptual systems, not with your thoughts
– but with your emotions and your body.

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:40.759
And that means you sweat, and you panic, and
you feel depressed, and you feel hurt, and

00:12:40.759 --> 00:12:41.759
you feel ashamed.

00:12:41.759 --> 00:12:45.459
And you’re prepared for a catastrophe, which
is stress.

00:12:45.459 --> 00:12:49.740
And all that’s basically non-cognitive and--
What that kind of means is that, when you

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:54.439
encounter something you don’t understand
the first manner in which you conceive of

00:12:54.439 --> 00:13:00.329
it, is embodied; emotional, physical, way
before you develop up an object representation,

00:13:00.329 --> 00:13:02.420
or a cognitive representation.

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:03.720
You may not ever get it.

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:08.059
Like, an event like that, or a worse event,
can throw someone into a tailspin that’s

00:13:08.059 --> 00:13:10.950
so extreme that they never get out of it.

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:14.699
So you’ll see, for example, sometimes – and
this is more true among elderly people if

00:13:14.699 --> 00:13:18.779
their spouse dies – the probability that
they’ll die in the next year - say, from

00:13:18.779 --> 00:13:22.410
a heart attack or something like that – increases
substantially.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:23.410
Why is that?

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:30.669
It’s because their conceptual frame was
so dependent on the existence of their spouse

00:13:30.669 --> 00:13:35.839
– say, someone they’ve lived with for
25 years – that the anxiety and uncertainty

00:13:35.839 --> 00:13:41.100
caused by their own anomalous disappearance
– their death is so extreme – that it

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:44.720
sends their body into a physiological state
that’s basically unbearable.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:45.939
And that does them in.

00:13:45.939 --> 00:13:51.459
And when you start to understand what having
your preconceptions rattled really means,

00:13:51.459 --> 00:13:57.929
then you also start to understand why people
are so motivated to protect their ideological

00:13:57.929 --> 00:14:01.440
territory, right – ideological territory--
that’s how you see the world.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:02.889
That’s your story.

00:14:02.889 --> 00:14:08.100
And you can’t have your fundaments rattled
all the time because it throws everything

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:13.860
into chaos, and that puts you in this terrible,
panicky, cortizol-ridden, stressful state

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:16.199
that’s really hard on you physically.

00:14:16.199 --> 00:14:20.329
So we know for example that if you’re in
a state where you’re chronically exposed

00:14:20.329 --> 00:14:25.040
to threat or punishment, which is the case
in depression, say, you produce a lot of cortizol,

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:28.110
which is a stress hormone, and cortizol is
toxic.

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:31.890
So the more of it you produce, the more you
kill off your hippocampus cells, and you really

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:35.410
need them because they’re a key to memory.

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:39.199
You do in your immunological system-- there’s
all sorts of negative side effects of cortizol

00:14:39.199 --> 00:14:43.529
poisoning - increase incidents of cardiovascular
disease, heightened rate of cancer.

00:14:43.529 --> 00:14:45.550
Plus, it’s just no fun, right?

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:48.660
It’s the worst thing that can possibly happen
to you, essentially.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:52.540
If something unknown happens to you and blows
your frame of reference, right – knocks

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:57.130
you for a loop, sends you to the underworld,
however you want to construe it – that’s

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:02.129
really going to upset your bandwagon and throw
you into a state that you’d do virtually

00:15:02.129 --> 00:15:03.379
anything to avoid.

00:15:03.379 --> 00:15:09.009
But by the same token, there is a possibility
that inside that chaotic nest, there’s something

00:15:09.009 --> 00:15:10.079
you really need.

00:15:10.079 --> 00:15:11.989
And what’s the logic there?

00:15:11.989 --> 00:15:16.509
Well, the logic is something like this: When
you look at the world, you only see a fragment

00:15:16.509 --> 00:15:17.509
of it.

00:15:17.509 --> 00:15:21.800
And that’s good because it’s pretty overwhelming,
and a fragment’s generally more than enough.

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:26.069
But all the information that you’ve ever
gathered in your entire life – to build

00:15:26.069 --> 00:15:30.730
yourself out of, and to make your life stable
– has come as a consequence of your ability

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:34.250
to explore what you don’t understand.

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:36.189
And that’s an unlimited capacity, right?

00:15:36.189 --> 00:15:40.420
No matter how much you explore and how much
information you gather, there’s always the

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:43.329
possibility that there’s way more information
out there.

00:15:43.329 --> 00:15:47.480
And that means, if you have a problem and
you see that it’s a problem, even though

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:52.759
that’s frightening, it’s also a gateway
into a domain of possibility; and the possibility

00:15:52.759 --> 00:15:57.709
is this richly informative background that
could, in principle, provide you with any

00:15:57.709 --> 00:16:01.899
answer you need
And then you can think one more thing.

00:16:01.899 --> 00:16:06.709
And the old gods, Mars say, the god of war.

00:16:06.709 --> 00:16:09.319
Venus, the god of love.

00:16:09.319 --> 00:16:11.739
They're all internalized for us, right?

00:16:11.739 --> 00:16:14.079
We know that anger's a psychological state.

00:16:14.079 --> 00:16:16.929
Love is a psychological state.

00:16:16.929 --> 00:16:22.720
But if we look at our great religious traditions,
Christianity say, or Buddhism, just to take

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:24.260
two as an example.

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:29.899
We still have this notion that what these
figures represent is something external.

00:16:29.899 --> 00:16:35.009
Well you might say how primitive, right?

00:16:35.009 --> 00:16:38.970
Just as primitive as the idea that the god
of war is something external is the idea that

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:41.550
this sort of figure is supposed to be something
external.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:44.870
It's supposed to be something embodied, right?

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:51.170
It’s a story about the nature of individual
moral responsibility

00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:59.100
So the idea is something like this, well reality
itself, the existence of things seems to depend

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:01.619
on the existence of a finite observer.

00:17:01.619 --> 00:17:03.389
So that we can see things from a perspective.

00:17:03.389 --> 00:17:06.490
If you don't see things from a perspective,
everything is the same.

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:09.290
There’s nothing delineated.

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:13.560
But if there's gonna be delineated things,
small things, insufficient things, and they're

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:18.250
gonna be aware, they're gonna be vulnerable
as a part of their limitation.

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:23.500
And so you say well, limitation is a precondition
for being.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:25.819
And that means suffering is part and parcel
of being

00:17:25.819 --> 00:17:35.370
Dostoevsky said clearly, look, I give you
all the cake you want.

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:36.580
You got a big house.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:39.110
You got nothing to do but watch TV, right?

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:41.020
And propagate the species.

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:42.410
Are you happy?

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:43.940
And Dostoevsky says well no.

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:44.940
Why?

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:49.770
Well cause human beings are really fundamentally
you know, ungrateful and insane.

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:53.520
So if you give them some little comfortable
niche to occupy themselves with so they don't

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:57.460
have anything to worry about, the first thing
they're gonna do just like Adam and Eve basically

00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:03.640
or just like Buddha, they're gonna run around
looking for the apple, looking for the snake,

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:08.320
looking for the trouble to smash the frame
into bits no matter how comfortable it is

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:11.950
just so they can get access to a little chaos
and have some fun.

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:16.160
And so then you think well maybe it's more
like the purpose of life isn't to avoid chaos

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:17.290
because we like chaos.

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:18.290
It’s entertaining, right?

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:19.500
It keeps us alert and awake.

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:22.830
And it gives us something to do that really
has no end.

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:26.960
And so maybe the answer is something more
like well forget the frame of reference.

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:31.570
And forget the chaos but hit the balance right
between the two, right?

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:34.390
So that you got one foot where it's reasonably
comfortable.

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:37.530
And you got one foot out there where it's
kind of exciting and dangerous.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:38.530
And that's perfect.

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:44.150
And then you think the state you wanna attain
that makes you resistant to even the greatest

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:50.040
anomalies, anomalies of death say or vulnerability
or mortality is exactly that, that position,

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:51.040
right?

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:55.910
Balanced right between the forces of chaos
and the forces of order or between, between

00:18:55.910 --> 00:18:57.280
yin and yang.

00:18:57.280 --> 00:18:59.170
And how do you know you're there?

00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:01.080
Cause that's what it really boils down to.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:02.760
How do you know that you're there?

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:06.180
And then you think okay, it's pretty simple.

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:10.430
You watch with your eyes open, just like Solzhenitsyn
watched.

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:12.780
You think, I don't know everything.

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:14.150
So let's see what I do know.

00:19:14.150 --> 00:19:15.390
No preconceptions.

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:20.550
I'm not gonna shield myself from the truth
with some second rate frame of reference.

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:23.600
We don't believe in those anyways cause they're
always fragmentable.

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.080
I'm just gonna watch.

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:26.810
So when am I not miserable?

00:19:26.810 --> 00:19:30.660
And then you think well, I'm not miserable
when I'm interested in thing, something, I

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:32.230
get interested in something.

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:35.120
I don't exactly know why I get interested
in it.

00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:36.690
It catches me.

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:43.020
And what are the, what's the phenomenology
of being caught?

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:46.010
I’m not self-conscious when I'm engaged
in something.

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:50.950
I'm more like a child, which is why children
have intonations of immortality.

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:53.760
I'm engaged in this process.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:56.160
I don't think about myself, so I’m not self-conscious.

00:19:56.160 --> 00:20:00.680
I lose my sense of temporality because it
seems like I can do whatever it is that I'm

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:03.260
doing, thing that I enjoy for hours.

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:04.550
And the time flies by.

00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:07.010
And I'm not even really aware of the surrounding
world.

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:11.800
And none of my existential concerns are paramount
at that time.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:15.680
Every need is suppressed by my engagement
in the activity.

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:19.720
And then you say to yourself well, yeah, that
only happens like you know, ten minutes every

00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:22.670
three days or something when I'm being particularly
miserable.

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:26.360
But you might say well the fact that it happens
at all is probably worth paying attention

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:27.360
to.

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:31.561
I mean if you believe that your experience
is real, like real, the fact that you can

00:20:31.561 --> 00:20:34.250
get into a state like that at all is worth
paying attention to.

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:37.910
And so then you might say well that's, that's
where you're sense of ethics really starts

00:20:37.910 --> 00:20:39.620
to arise.

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:40.810
what makes you interested?

00:20:40.810 --> 00:20:45.190
Well it might be just as cracked and, and,
and peculiar as something you could possibly

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:46.190
imagine, right?

00:20:46.190 --> 00:20:47.370
Your parents are against it.

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:48.960
Your friends are against it.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:50.990
Even you’re against it when you’re thinking
clearly.

00:20:50.990 --> 00:20:55.640
But there's still this reality that something
compels you.

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:58.300
And then you think well can you trust it?

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:03.360
And I think well that's a tough question because
I read a long time, for a long time I read

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:04.830
accounts of serial killers.

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:08.310
Cause I was really interested in what motivated
them, right?

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:12.330
And they’re an interesting breed in many
ways, which is why there's such a popular

00:21:12.330 --> 00:21:13.930
fascination with them.

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:19.720
And you think geez, maybe you can't trust
your interest, right?

00:21:19.720 --> 00:21:23.520
Maybe it'll take you somewhere you don't wanna
go, like seriously where you don't wanna go.

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:28.680
How do you know that if you really let yourself
be who you could be, that you'd end up somewhere

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:29.680
good.

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:34.360
And so then you come to the second part of
the story, which is something like this; let's

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:40.080
say that you’re a very, very, very finely
tuned biological machine, right?

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:42.710
Akin to a computer.

00:21:42.710 --> 00:21:48.080
And then you say okay, you take a computer
and you feed it false information.

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:50.080
What do you get out?

00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:52.510
False information, right?

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:54.270
Well you’re stuck with this computer.

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:55.960
It's very complicated.

00:21:55.960 --> 00:22:00.090
You kind of reside in it in some manner you
don't understand.

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:05.900
And yet, you’re prone on occasions either
to deny the information altogether when you

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:08.810
walk away from something you know you shouldn’t
walk from.

00:22:08.810 --> 00:22:10.910
Or to feed it bad information.

00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:12.840
And so then what if it, what if this was the
case?

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:18.130
What if it was the case that the systems that
orient you with regards to your interest can

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:23.260
become pathologized by any relationship you
have with yourself that's predicated on bad

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:24.440
faith?

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:30.240
And so then you think well, that's why there's
an ethical aspect to this redemptive process.

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:34.350
Like a real strenuous and strict ethical aspect
that goes something like this;

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:39.800
There are things that you can do, find yourself
engaged with the world at such a level that

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:42.490
your existential concerns could disappear.

00:22:42.490 --> 00:22:46.470
And we can even understand that biochemically
to some degree because if you're really interested

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:50.590
in something you got a dopamine release an
exploratory dopamine release.

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:51.590
That’s great.

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:56.370
I mean that's associated with positive affect,
with confidence, with increased immunological

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:58.690
functioning, with better memory functioning.

00:22:58.690 --> 00:22:59.990
With learning.

00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:02.570
Everything you want.

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:08.850
It's also potently anxiolytic and analgesic,
which means that if you're really pursuing

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:13.090
something that's compelling to you, you're
much more resistant biochemically to punishment,

00:23:13.090 --> 00:23:15.530
disappointment, depression, pain, threat,
etcetera.

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:19.320
And it's not cause you're blind.

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:20.320
It's not cause you're blind.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:24.180
It's cause your nervous system is optimally
tuned to make you maximally resistant.

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:30.130
And so then you might think if you were optimally
tuned, how resistant would you be?

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:31.430
You don't know, right?

00:23:31.430 --> 00:23:36.790
Cause it's in a spiral that never stops moving
uphill.

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:39.000
We don't know what the upper end is.

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:43.770
And we have exemplars that might indicate
what that upper end could be.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:45.900
But we don't actually know.

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:52.030
So then you think okay, well here's the here's
the rule, say, something like this.

00:23:52.030 --> 00:23:55.830
If you look at the world from this perspective,
which is something you have to decide if you're

00:23:55.830 --> 00:24:05.980
you know, you find compelling and reasonable,
the rule is this; you're always gonna run

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:07.790
into anomaly, right?

00:24:07.790 --> 00:24:13.660
And anomaly’s always gonna look, look to
you like, like this.

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:15.380
And
it's no bloody wonder you wanna run away from

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:16.380
that.

00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:20.040
I mean in some ways your whole body's telling
you watch out, and for good reason because

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:21.160
it's no joke.

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:25.510
But then there's more to the story because
the anomalous thing, that's everything you

00:24:25.510 --> 00:24:27.600
don’t know.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:30.860
you might say well, you're going out with
someone and you wanna have a long term relationship.

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:31.860
They betray you.

00:24:31.860 --> 00:24:34.790
How could there be any good in that?

00:24:34.790 --> 00:24:39.590
That's certainly what you're gonna ask when
you first encounter the unexpected information

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:44.660
But then you might think could be that I'm
a little too naive for my own good.

00:24:44.660 --> 00:24:45.660
Right?

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:49.630
People pull me in a little more than they
should or I'm not sufficiently careful when

00:24:49.630 --> 00:24:51.670
I enter into a relationship with people.

00:24:51.670 --> 00:24:53.140
Or I don't treat people right.

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:56.340
Or I don't have a good conceptualization of
myself.

00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:59.140
Or I'm chasing after the wrong person.

00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:01.300
Well that's all gonna be very annoying to
learn.

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:03.390
But if you don't learn it you're gonna be
in big trouble.

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:08.330
So maybe the best thing to do when an anomaly
of that sort hits you is to think okay, yeah,

00:25:08.330 --> 00:25:09.340
it's a dragon.

00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:11.290
No doubt it will eat me.

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:14.540
But if I don't let it eat me then there'll
just be another one waiting around the corner

00:25:14.540 --> 00:25:16.080
and it'll probably be a little bit bigger.

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:19.960
And if I get eaten by enough of them, I'm
not really gonna wanna be around much.

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:23.230
And maybe I'm not gonna be willing to help
other people be around much either.

00:25:23.230 --> 00:25:28.410
Doesn’t seem like a very good alternative
to me.

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:38.150
So back in 1957, some new Gnostic writings
were discovered in a, in a cave.

00:25:38.150 --> 00:25:43.660
They were discovered by these two Arab guys
who went out to kill the man who killed their

00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:44.660
father.

00:25:44.660 --> 00:25:46.030
They took him out into this cave and they
killed him.

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:50.930
And when they were burying him they found
these amphora full of papers and papyrus.

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:51.930
And they took them home.

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:55.080
And their mother used a bunch of them to light
cooking fires with.

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:58.650
And one day one of their friends who was an
antiquities dealer showed up.

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:01.030
And he said you know you shouldn't be burning
those.

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:03.630
Those are about fifteen hundred years old.

00:26:03.630 --> 00:26:08.630
And they look like very early Gnostic gospels
And the Gnostics were this branch of Christianity

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:12.960
that was pretty violently suppressed by the
emergence of Orthodox Christianity.

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:18.560
And the Gnostics believed that faith was a
good thing but knowledge was all right too.

00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:25.650
And they wrote gospel accounts say of Christ's
life that were knowledge predicated as much

00:26:25.650 --> 00:26:27.450
as revelation predicated say.

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:30.800
And this is one of the quotes from one of
those gospels, the gospel of Thomas, which

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:34.470
is actually one that, the only one that got
out.

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:36.860
Carl Jung got a hold of them, interestingly
enough.

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:38.750
And this is one of the quotes.

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:40.910
And I really like this.

00:26:40.910 --> 00:26:43.570
This is a non-canonical saying of Christ.

00:26:43.570 --> 00:26:49.990
And the saying is: “if you bring forth what
is within you what you bring forth will save

00:26:49.990 --> 00:26:51.230
you.

00:26:51.230 --> 00:26:59.120
If you do not bring forth what is within you,
what you do not bring forth will destroy you.”

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:04.490
And I think that's a pretty good line to close
off the class.

00:27:04.490 --> 00:27:06.600
So thank you for attending.

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:06.820
(clapping) (End)

